<p>A bicycle is a nearly perfect machine. Not because it is complicated, but because every part has earned its place. I keep thinking about that when I open a codebase that has forgotten how to be simple.</p>

<p>My bike has two wheels, a frame, a chain, and a handful of things that make it stop. When something feels wrong, the problem is usually obvious once you look closely. There is nowhere for a bad decision to hide.</p>

<h2>Earned complexity</h2>

<p>Software is different because layers can hide inside other layers. A feature becomes a service. A service becomes a platform. A platform becomes a team. Soon nobody can tell where the noise is coming from, so we add more layers to manage the noise.</p>

<p>Good engineering is not the elimination of complexity. That is impossible. It is the willingness to ask, part by part, whether this complexity is earning its keep. Does this abstraction make the system easier to understand, or merely easier to talk about?</p>

<p>A bike is not simple because it was designed by a minimalist. It is simple because a hundred years of riders kept noticing what did not need to be there. Software could use more riders.</p>
